WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.760
ED GLAESER: Many of humanity's greatest hits have occurred in cities.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:07.190
Penicillin in London, the telephone in Boston, Facebook in Cambridge, Mass.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.810
But the technological breakthroughs of Silicon Valley had being a decidedly

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.570
suburban affair.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:15.250
While Silicon Valley is certainly dense with talent and

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.700
its companies value face to face interactions.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.910
Those interactions take place in a car oriented, moderate

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.920
density at most semi-urban cluster nestled between San Francisco and San Jose.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.350
Only during this millenium have we seen a real trend where many tech related

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:33.080
startups have shown up in cities like San Francisco, Boston, and New York.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.810
Timothy Sturgeon's work teaches us that the history of Silicon Valley

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:39.430
starts well before World War II.

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:43.590
One of the region's first tech start ups was the federal telegraph

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:47.400
which was founded because of a teenage radio genius, Francis McCarthy

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.880
of San Francisco, who died in a freak streetcar accident in that city.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.060
The streetcar killed the radio star.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.140
McCarthy's financial supporters decided that wireless telegraphy still had promise

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.460
and then he turned to a Stanford electrical engineer, Harris Ryan.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.200
He recommended a star pupil of his, Cyril Elwell, to develop and

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.560
commercialize McCarthy's ideas.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.370
Stanford University was founded by railroad builder and

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:15.120
California governor, Leland Stanford, as a deeply practical place of learning.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.770
Business and science were woven together deep in Stanford's DNA from the beginning

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.140
and that helps explain why this played such a crucial role in the development of

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:24.805
Silicon Valley.

00:01:24.805 --> 00:01:26.840
Elwell dropped McCarty's plans and

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:30.690
turned instead to Valdemar Poulsen's arc transmitter.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.230
And Federal Telegraph began as an American Outpost of that Danish Technology.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:39.850
From 1911 to 1913, Lee de Forest worked at Federal Telegraph,

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.920
where he developed revolutionary vacuum tube amplifiers and

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.390
oscillators that would dominate the radio industry for years.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.370
Federal Telegraph inspired and then employed the young Fred Terman,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:54.460
the son of a Stanford University psychologist who pioneered IQ tests.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.449
Young Fred studied engineering at Stanford and MIT and

00:01:57.449 --> 00:02:01.281
eventually returned to Stanford as Professor Dean and Provost.

00:02:01.281 --> 00:02:04.857
Terman saw the advantage of industry university interactions just like Stanford

00:02:04.857 --> 00:02:07.250
himself and the investors who contacted Harris Ryan.

00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:10.086
And he planned an industrial park right near the campus.

00:02:10.086 --> 00:02:14.310
Such commercialization was anathema to many older Eastern universities as late as

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.670
the 1980s.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.520
For an anchor tenant he reached out to William Shockley,

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.970
the Nobel Prize winning co-inventor of the semi-conductor,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:25.940
who was willing to come to Silicon Valley partly because he grew up in Palo Alto and

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.900
knew its charms, especially its mild Mediterrean climate.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.560
Shockley proved to be the ideal

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.440
entrepreneur to jumpstart a regional innovation economy.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.810
His genius attracted talent.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.710
Shockley Semiconductors' early employees are now legendary.

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:44.210
Gordon Moore of Moore's Law, Eugene Kleiner of Kleiner-Perkins,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.120
Robert Noyce of Intel.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.560
Then his erratic management style repulsed that talent and

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.000
sent it out throughout the Valley to form their own firms.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.800
Eight brilliant employees, "the traitorous eight," left Shockley Semiconductor

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.392
in 1957 to be part of a new company, Fairchild Semiconductor.

00:03:02.392 --> 00:03:04.820
Fairchild Semiconductor then generated more spin offs,

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:08.615
the Fair Children and they came to be at the economic heart of Silicon Valley.

00:03:08.615 --> 00:03:13.730
AnnaLee Saxenian's regional advantage compares the corporate cultures in Silicon Valley

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.160
and the tech district in Boston's route 128.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.130
In her telling, Boston was hierarchical, corporate,

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:21.855
and dominated by a few big companies such as Raytheon.

00:03:21.855 --> 00:03:25.500
Which had been founded Vannevar Bush, Termin's MIT mentor.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.730
Those big companies didn't talk to each other.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:32.360
By contrast, Silicon Valley's culture was entrepreneurial and interactive.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.660
Saxenian describes how bars like Walkers Wagon Wheel became hubs for

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:38.180
idea exchange in the valley in the 1960s.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.690
Silicon Valley ate route 128 for

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.960
lunch which reflects a more general fact of that post war economic growth.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:47.700
Entrepreneurial places with lots of little firms have done much

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.630
better than places dominated by a few big companies.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:51.710
There maybe a lesson in that for

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.609
city officials who try to woo mega employers with generous subsidies.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:58.870
In a sense, Silicon Valley did benefit from a traditional

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:01.220
urban asset of face to face contact.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:04.270
And it certainly benefited from the proximity to all that engineering

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:05.990
brilliance at Stanford.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.310
But it did keep it's success at a suburban density level.

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:12.590
Clearly, you don't need density to be economically dynamic.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:15.920
At least if you've got a great university, a great climate, and

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:18.420
you can attract many of the most innovative people on the planet.

00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:22.240
One downside of the Silicon Valley model is

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.770
that it is a bit of an industrial monoculture.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:26.150
Whereas technology in New York or

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:30.600
London exists in the midst of a diverse urban economy, Silicon Valley does not.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:33.860
The great urbanist Jane Jacobs argued that industrial diversity

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.130
often allows new ideas to be created by combining old ideas.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.930
Innovation occurs by cross industry leaps of imagination.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:45.180
Michael Bloomberg's company, which is ultimately a tech firm itself,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.690
was created in New York, not Silicon Valley,

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:51.910
precisely because Bloomberg knew what Wall Street traders wanted on their desks.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:55.700
Gradually tech companies started rediscovering the virtues of dense, messy,

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:56.750
urban life.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.730
Amazon relocated to the urban heart of Seattle.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.130
Pinterest, Zynga, Trulia, and

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:04.900
Yelp are all in the city of San Francisco itself, not in Silicon Valley.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:06.160
So is Uber.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.000
Indeed, it's hard to imagine that car service company starting in a suburb.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.000
It's success depended on having enough density of drivers and customers.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.070
Wayfair and Akamai in Boston.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.080
New York has a legion of tech companies and some of them like E*trade and Tumblr,

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:23.940
seem to draw on the city's traditional industries like finance and publishing.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.985
Google's parent company, Alphabet, has an Invested heavily in New York,

00:05:26.985 --> 00:05:30.135
both through Google itself and through its investment in Sidewalk Labs.

00:05:31.325 --> 00:05:34.075
Why have tech companies started to like cities?

00:05:34.075 --> 00:05:37.505
In many cases, the urban location reflects the preferences of the owners and

00:05:37.505 --> 00:05:38.792
their employees.

00:05:38.792 --> 00:05:41.532
For younger, hipper people, the suburbs, even Silicon Valley,

00:05:41.532 --> 00:05:42.852
may not seem like that much fun.

00:05:43.962 --> 00:05:48.582
Zappos's move to Los Vegas gave it access to a very special type of labor.

00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:52.150
The highly theatrical, strongly outgoing people who answer its phone lines.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.790
And try to bind customers to the company.

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:59.030
Biogen relocated its headquarters from the leafy Boston suburb where I usually sleep

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.370
to Kendall Square, which is closer to its academic partners at MIT.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.870
In this case, the move reflects a recognition of the traditional

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:08.490
urban advantage in spreading knowledge.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:12.100
In some cases, urbanization is a good fit for the product mix.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.270
When a tech company provides services which are particularly valuable in cities

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:20.100
like Yelp or Uber, it probably makes sense to better understand urban life.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.980
Certainly, knowledge of the difficulties of urban existence

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.780
can help give entrepreneurs good ideas, as it did for Robin Chase at Zipcar.

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.610
Silicon Valley is going great guns.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.870
And I'm not predicting that this will change.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:35.310
But it's worth noting that a century ago such a successful place would

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.550
have turned itself into a real city.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.170
Unfettered by land use regulations, demand for

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.900
the area space would have lead to soaring densities.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.490
Without zoning rules Silicon Valley streets would be lined with

00:06:45.490 --> 00:06:48.310
skyscrapers just like New York City.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:52.160
I'm sure many of the valley's residents are glad that that hasn't happened.

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:54.520
And that the region has retained its character.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:55.780
But it's worth stressing that for

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.940
all the valley's creative strength, it has one great failure.

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.980
It is ridiculously expensive.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.100
It just does not supply anywhere near the number of homes needed to keep

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.770
pace with demand.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.840
If the region chooses to remain low density, its housing prices will stay high

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.810
and it will never provide welcoming space for middle income people.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.690
The way that the growing cities of New York and Chicago did a century ago.

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:20.060
Silicon Valley's restrictiveness and high prices may make it a paradise for

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.770
some of the smartest Americans.

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:23.355
But by restricting the growth of density,

00:07:23.355 --> 00:07:25.950
it fails to provide more widespread opportunity.

